export const exp2Materials: AuthoredMaterial[] = [
  { id: "cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-m01", topicId: "cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional", title: "Cognitive Errors vs Emotional Biases", content: `Behavioral biases split into **cognitive errors** (faulty reasoning) and **emotional biases** (feelings). Cognitive errors are easier to correct with education and data; emotional biases are harder and are usually **accommodated**.

## Cognitive errors
- **Belief perseverance**: conservatism, confirmation, representativeness, illusion of control, hindsight.
- **Information processing**: anchoring/adjustment, mental accounting, framing, availability.

## Emotional biases
- **Loss aversion**, **overconfidence**, **self-control**, **status quo**, **endowment**, **regret aversion**.

## Practical response
- **Moderate** or **adapt** to a bias depending on the client's wealth relative to their standard of living and the bias type.
- Emotional biases are generally accommodated; cognitive errors are corrected.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-m01", topicId: "cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory", title: "Behavioral Portfolio Theory & Goals", content: `**Behavioral portfolio theory (BPT)** says investors build portfolios as **layered pyramids**, each layer meeting a goal with its own risk attitude — rather than one mean-variance optimal portfolio.

## Key ideas
- Investors hold a **low-risk layer** (safety) and **high-risk layer** (aspiration) simultaneously, ignoring correlations across layers.
- This mirrors **mental accounting** and **goals-based investing**.

## Consequences
- Resulting portfolios may be **inefficient** relative to the mean-variance frontier.
- Advisers can harness this by framing allocations around **client goals** (near-term needs vs long-term aspirations), improving adherence even if slightly suboptimal.

BPT helps explain why real portfolios differ from the single tangency portfolio of modern portfolio theory.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-m01", topicId: "cfa3-behavioral-anomalies", title: "Behavioral Explanations of Anomalies", content: `Market **anomalies** appear to contradict market efficiency; behavioral finance offers explanations.

## Common anomalies
- **Momentum**: winners keep winning short-term — linked to **herding** and under-reaction.
- **Value/size effects**: cheap and small stocks outperform — possibly risk or mispricing.
- **Calendar effects** (January effect), **overreaction** and long-term reversals.
- **Bubbles and crashes**: driven by herding, overconfidence and regret.

## Cautions
- Some anomalies vanish after discovery (arbitraged away) or reflect **data mining**.
- Others may be **compensation for risk** rather than true inefficiency.

Behavioral biases (overconfidence, anchoring, herding) provide plausible mechanisms, but rational risk-based explanations must also be considered.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-m01", topicId: "cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework", title: "CME Framework & Challenges", content: `**Capital market expectations (CME)** are forecasts of risk and return for asset classes, feeding strategic allocation.

## A disciplined framework
1. Specify the expectations needed and the horizon.
2. Research historical/current conditions.
3. Select forecasting models and gather data.
4. Interpret and formulate expectations; monitor and adjust.

## Common problems
- **Limitations of economic data** (revisions, lags), **data-measurement errors and biases** (survivorship, appraisal smoothing).
- **The limits of historical estimates** and **non-stationarity** (regime changes).
- **Ex-post risk as a biased measure of ex-ante risk**, **psychological biases** (anchoring, overconfidence) and **model/input uncertainty**.

Good CME require combining statistical tools with sound economic judgement.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-m01", topicId: "cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages", title: "Macro Linkages & Emerging Markets", content: `Economies are linked through **trade and capital flows**, so business cycles and policy spill across borders.

## Linkages
- **Inflation/deflation** and monetary policy affect real rates and currencies.
- Countries with strong trade/capital ties tend to have **more correlated** cycles.

## Emerging markets — extra risks
- **Political and legal** risk (weaker property rights, unstable policy).
- **Economic** risk (commodity dependence, fiscal/current-account deficits, high debt).
- **Currency** risk and possible capital controls.

## Forecasting approaches
- **Econometric models**, **leading indicators**, and **checklist** approaches.
- Assess an emerging economy's growth via factors like savings/investment, demographics, and structural policy.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-capital-market-expectations-risk-premium-models-m01", topicId: "cfa3-capital-market-expectations-risk-premium-models", title: "Discount-Rate & Risk-Premium Models", content: `Expected returns are often built as a **risk-free rate plus risk premia**.

## Building blocks
- **Bond returns** ≈ real risk-free rate + inflation premium + default premium + illiquidity premium + maturity (term) premium.
- **Equity returns** via the **Grinold–Kroner** model: expected return ≈ dividend yield − Δshares + inflation + real earnings growth + Δ(P/E).

## Other tools
- **Building-block / risk-premium** approach sums compensations for each risk.
- **DCF (Gordon growth)** implies expected return = D₁/P + g.
- **Singer–Terhaar** (extended ICAPM) blends fully segmented and fully integrated market expectations using a degree of integration.

These models translate macro views into asset-class return forecasts.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-asset-allocation-risk-budgeting-m01", topicId: "cfa3-asset-allocation-risk-budgeting", title: "Risk Budgeting in Allocation", content: `**Risk budgeting** allocates a portfolio's total risk across positions or factors, rather than allocating capital alone.

## Concepts
- **Marginal contribution to risk (MCTR)** = sensitivity of portfolio risk to a small increase in a position.
- **Absolute contribution to risk (ACTR)** = weight × MCTR; the ACTRs sum to total portfolio risk.
- An allocation is **optimal** when the **ratio of excess return to MCTR is equal** across positions.

## Uses
- Ensures risk isn't unintentionally concentrated (e.g., 'equity' dominating a balanced fund's risk).
- **Risk parity** equalizes risk contributions across assets, often adding leverage to low-risk assets.

Risk budgeting complements return forecasting by focusing on where risk is actually taken.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-asset-allocation-currency-m01", topicId: "cfa3-asset-allocation-currency", title: "Currency Management in Allocation", content: `Global portfolios carry **currency exposure** that can be managed separately from asset selection.

## Strategic choices
- **Currency-neutral** (fully hedged) to **unhedged** are the poles; most managers sit in between with a **strategic hedge ratio**.
- Rationale for hedging: currencies add volatility but little expected return over the long run; hedging costs and manager skill matter.

## Tools & tactics
- **Forwards** and **currency options**; **cross-hedges** and **proxy hedges** when direct hedges are costly.
- **Minimum-variance hedge ratio** from regressing asset returns on currency returns.
- Active managers may take currency views via carry, value (PPP) and momentum, treating currency as an alpha source.

Discretion is set in the IPS's currency-risk policy.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-asset-allocation-tactical-m01", topicId: "cfa3-asset-allocation-tactical", title: "Tactical Asset Allocation", content: `**Tactical asset allocation (TAA)** makes short-term deviations from the strategic (policy) weights to exploit perceived opportunities.

## Approaches
- **Discretionary TAA** relies on manager judgement about markets.
- **Systematic TAA** uses rules/signals such as value, momentum and carry.

## Considerations
- TAA is measured against the strategic policy portfolio; success is judged by **information ratio** of the tactical bets.
- Costs: **transaction costs**, **taxes**, and higher **tracking error** versus policy.
- Constraints from the IPS bound how far weights may deviate.

TAA can add value but introduces active risk; it must be disciplined and cost-aware.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-fixed-income-pm-liquidity-m01", topicId: "cfa3-fixed-income-pm-liquidity", title: "Bond Market Liquidity & Trading", content: `Bond markets are largely **over-the-counter** and far less liquid than equities.

## Liquidity features
- Most trading is **dealer-intermediated**; liquidity varies hugely by sector (governments most liquid; high-yield and structured least).
- **Newly issued (on-the-run)** bonds are more liquid than **off-the-run**.
- Liquidity dries up in stress, widening bid-ask spreads.

## Managing liquidity
- Hold liquid instruments (governments, futures) as a **liquidity buffer**.
- Use **credit default swaps** and **bond futures** to adjust exposure without trading cash bonds.
- Trading costs are largely **implicit** (bid-ask, market impact) rather than explicit commissions.

Liquidity risk is a key consideration in fixed-income portfolio construction.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-fixed-income-pm-inflation-linked-m01", topicId: "cfa3-fixed-income-pm-inflation-linked", title: "Inflation-Linked Bonds & Real Return", content: `**Inflation-linked bonds** (e.g., TIPS) protect purchasing power by tying principal or coupons to a price index.

## Mechanics
- Principal accretes with inflation; coupons are paid on the adjusted principal.
- The yield quoted is a **real yield**; the gap between nominal and real yields is the **breakeven inflation rate**.

## Uses
- Hedge **inflation risk** for liabilities linked to prices (pensions, endowments' spending).
- Low correlation with nominal bonds when inflation surprises occur.

## Risks
- **Real-rate risk** remains (duration), and **deflation** can hurt (though many have a principal floor).
- Liquidity and tax treatment (phantom income on accretion) are practical concerns.

Breakeven inflation is a market-based inflation expectation used in CME.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-fixed-income-pm-international-m01", topicId: "cfa3-fixed-income-pm-international", title: "International Bond Investing & Hedging", content: `Global bonds add **diversification** but introduce **currency** and **cross-market** risk.

## Return components
- Local-currency bond return + **currency return** (spot change plus any hedging carry).
- Fully hedging currency converts the foreign yield roughly into the domestic-cash yield plus the foreign bond's excess return.

## Strategies
- **Interest-rate differentials** and expected currency moves drive country allocation.
- **Duration** can be taken in the most attractive markets, with currency hedged back if desired.
- The **hedged return** relationship is governed by covered interest rate parity.

## Considerations
- Diversification benefit depends on low correlation of global rate cycles.
- Emerging-market debt adds credit and political risk but higher yield.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-performance-gips-m01", topicId: "cfa3-performance-gips", title: "GIPS & Performance Presentation", content: `The **Global Investment Performance Standards (GIPS)** standardize how firms present investment results.

## Core requirements
- Compliance is **firm-wide**; no partial compliance.
- Group portfolios into **composites** by strategy, including **all actual, fee-paying, discretionary** portfolios.
- Returns are **time-weighted**; show at least **5 years** building to 10.

## Presentation
- Disclose benchmark, fees (gross/net), **composite dispersion**, and number of portfolios.
- **Verification** is firm-wide and voluntary but recommended, adding credibility.

## Why it matters
GIPS makes performance **comparable across managers** and prevents cherry-picking, supporting fair client decisions.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-performance-macro-micro-m01", topicId: "cfa3-performance-macro-micro", title: "Macro vs Micro Attribution", content: `Attribution decomposes performance into its **sources**.

## Macro attribution
- Applied at the **fund-sponsor** level; attributes total fund return to decisions such as net contributions, the policy (benchmark) allocation, asset-category allocation, and manager selection.

## Micro attribution
- Applied at the **individual portfolio/manager** level.
- **Brinson–Hood–Beebower**: splits active return into **allocation** (over/underweighting sectors), **selection** (picking within sectors), and an **interaction** term.

## Fixed-income attribution
- Decomposes return into **duration/curve** effects, **spread** changes and **selection**.

Choosing macro vs micro depends on whether the sponsor or the individual manager is being evaluated.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-performance-risk-adjusted-m01", topicId: "cfa3-performance-risk-adjusted", title: "Risk-Adjusted Performance Measures", content: `Risk-adjusted measures compare return **per unit of risk**.

## Common ratios
| Measure | Risk used |
| --- | --- |
| **Sharpe** | total risk (σ) |
| **Treynor** | systematic risk (β) |
| **Information ratio** | active risk (tracking error) |
| **Sortino** | downside deviation |

- **Jensen's alpha** = actual return − CAPM-required return.
- **M² (Modigliani)** restates the Sharpe ratio as a benchmark-relative return.
- **Appraisal ratio** = alpha / non-systematic risk.

## Choosing a measure
- Use **Sharpe** for a standalone portfolio; **Treynor/Jensen** when the portfolio is one of many (only systematic risk matters); **information ratio** to judge active management.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-equity-pm-index-construction-m01", topicId: "cfa3-equity-pm-index-construction", title: "Index Construction & Weighting", content: `Equity indexes differ by **weighting scheme**, which shapes their risk and turnover.

## Weighting methods
- **Market-cap weighted**: reflects the investable opportunity set; low turnover but tilts to large, possibly overvalued names.
- **Price weighted** (e.g., some legacy indexes): higher-priced stocks dominate — largely an artifact.
- **Equal weighted**: more small-cap and value tilt; higher turnover from rebalancing.
- **Fundamental weighted**: weights by sales, earnings or book — breaks the price link, adding a value tilt.

## Implications for passive investors
- Choice of index defines the passive exposure; **reconstitution** and **rebalancing** create turnover and tracking considerations.
- Full replication vs **stratified sampling** vs **optimization** are the tracking methods.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-equity-pm-long-short-m01", topicId: "cfa3-equity-pm-long-short", title: "Long/Short & Market-Neutral Equity", content: `Long/short strategies buy expected winners and **short** expected losers.

## Structures
- **Market-neutral**: balance long and short betas to ≈ 0, isolating stock selection (alpha) from market direction.
- **Long-biased (e.g., 130/30)**: 130% long, 30% short — retains market exposure while adding shorting to express negative views.
- **Pairs trading**: long/short two related securities to capture relative mispricing.

## Considerations
- Shorting adds **borrowing costs**, potential **unlimited loss**, and **short-squeeze** risk.
- Enables a higher **transfer coefficient** by acting on negative signals (fundamental law of active management).
- Leverage and financing must be managed carefully.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-equity-pm-costs-fees-m01", topicId: "cfa3-equity-pm-costs-fees", title: "Equity Costs, Fees & Turnover", content: `Costs erode active returns and must be managed.

## Types of cost
- **Explicit**: commissions, fees, taxes.
- **Implicit**: **bid-ask spread**, **market impact**, and **delay/opportunity cost** — together the **implementation shortfall**.

## Turnover and taxes
- Higher **turnover** raises trading costs and, for taxable investors, realizes gains sooner (tax drag).
- Tax-aware strategies use **loss harvesting** and holding-period management.

## Fees
- Management fees plus performance fees; evaluate net-of-fee alpha.
- A strategy with modest gross alpha can deliver **negative net alpha** after costs, so cost control is itself a source of value.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-risk-management-exposure-adjustment-m01", topicId: "cfa3-risk-management-exposure-adjustment", title: "Adjusting Exposure with Futures", content: `Derivatives let managers change exposure quickly and cheaply without trading the underlying.

## Equity beta adjustment
- Number of futures = ((β_target − β_current)/β_futures) × (Portfolio value / (futures price × multiplier)).
- To **equitize cash**, buy index futures to gain equity exposure on a cash balance.

## Duration adjustment
- Use bond futures to move portfolio duration toward a target: contracts ≈ ((D_target − D_current) × Portfolio value) / (D_futures × futures price).

## Uses
- Tactical shifts, transition management, and hedging.
- Futures are capital-efficient (margin only) and highly liquid, but require managing basis risk and rolls.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-risk-management-fx-hedging-m01", topicId: "cfa3-risk-management-fx-hedging", title: "Currency Hedging Strategies", content: `Managers hedge currency to reduce volatility or take active currency views.

## Instruments
- **Forwards** (most common), **currency futures**, **options**, and **cross-currency swaps**.
- **Options** provide asymmetric protection (a floor) at the cost of a premium.

## Hedge ratios
- Range from **0% (unhedged)** to **100% (fully hedged)**; a **minimum-variance hedge ratio** may differ from 1 due to correlation between the asset and currency.
- **Dynamic hedging** adjusts the ratio as markets move.

## Active currency tactics
- **Carry** (buy high-yield/sell low-yield), **value** (PPP-based), and **momentum**.
- Roll yield on forwards and hedging cost depend on interest-rate differentials.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-risk-management-tail-risk-m01", topicId: "cfa3-risk-management-tail-risk", title: "Tail Risk & Stress Testing", content: `**Tail risk** is the risk of rare, extreme losses that normal (VaR) models understate.

## Measures beyond VaR
- **Conditional VaR (expected shortfall)**: the average loss **given** the VaR threshold is breached — captures tail severity.
- VaR tells you a threshold; CVaR tells you how bad it gets beyond it.

## Techniques
- **Stress testing** applies severe but plausible shocks (rate spikes, equity crashes).
- **Scenario analysis** models specific historical or hypothetical events.
- **Reverse stress testing** starts from a failure and finds what could cause it.

## Managing tail risk
- **Tail hedges** (out-of-the-money puts), diversification across truly uncorrelated assets, and holding liquidity buffers.
- Fat tails and rising correlations in crises make tail risk larger than a normal model implies.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-private-wealth-concentrated-m01", topicId: "cfa3-private-wealth-concentrated", title: "Managing Concentrated Positions", content: `Wealthy clients often hold a **concentrated single-stock** or private-business position, creating outsized idiosyncratic risk.

## Objectives & constraints
- Goals: **reduce risk**, **generate liquidity**, and **defer/minimize tax** — often in tension.
- Constraints: capital-gains tax, restricted/legacy shares, emotional attachment, control considerations.

## Strategies
- **Outright sale** (simple but taxable).
- **Equity monetization**: a **collar** (buy put, sell call) plus borrowing against the position to raise cash while deferring tax.
- **Exchange funds** pool concentrated holdings for diversification with tax deferral.
- **Charitable trusts** (CRTs) and **completion portfolios** to diversify around the position.

The right approach balances tax efficiency against risk reduction and the client's goals.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-private-wealth-family-governance-m01", topicId: "cfa3-private-wealth-family-governance", title: "Family Governance & Education", content: `**Family governance** structures how a wealthy family makes decisions and transfers values across generations.

## Elements
- A **family mission statement** and **constitution** articulate shared goals and rules.
- **Family councils/meetings** provide a forum for communication and conflict resolution.
- **Education** prepares heirs to be responsible stewards of wealth.

## Why it matters
- Most multigenerational wealth erosion stems from **breakdowns in communication and trust**, not poor investing.
- Good governance addresses the classic 'shirtsleeves to shirtsleeves in three generations' risk.

## Family office
- A **single-family office** or **multi-family office** coordinates investments, tax, estate and philanthropy for the family.

Governance complements the financial plan by managing the human/relational side of wealth.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-private-wealth-philanthropy-m01", topicId: "cfa3-private-wealth-philanthropy", title: "Charitable Giving & Philanthropy", content: `Philanthropy achieves personal goals while often providing **tax efficiency**.

## Vehicles
- **Direct gifts** of cash or **appreciated securities** (avoids capital-gains tax and gives a deduction).
- **Donor-advised funds**: immediate deduction, grants over time.
- **Private foundations**: more control, higher administration and payout rules.
- **Charitable remainder trusts (CRT)**: income to the donor now, remainder to charity — useful for concentrated, low-basis stock.
- **Charitable lead trusts (CLT)**: income to charity first, remainder to heirs.

## Planning
- Gifting **appreciated assets** is usually more tax-efficient than gifting cash then selling.
- Coordinate philanthropy with the overall **estate plan** and the family's values/governance.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-institutional-dc-pensions-m01", topicId: "cfa3-institutional-dc-pensions", title: "Defined-Contribution Plans", content: `In a **defined-contribution (DC)** plan, the **employee bears the investment risk**, unlike a defined-benefit plan where the sponsor does.

## Features
- Benefits depend on contributions and investment returns; there is **no guaranteed payout**.
- The plan sponsor's duties focus on offering a **suitable menu**, controlling fees, and fiduciary oversight.

## Design considerations
- **Default options** (target-date funds) leverage inertia to improve outcomes.
- **Auto-enrollment** and **auto-escalation** raise participation and savings.
- Address participant **behavioral biases** (inertia, naive diversification, employer-stock over-concentration).

Compared with DB, DC shifts **longevity and market risk** to individuals, raising the importance of good defaults and education.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-institutional-endowment-model-m01", topicId: "cfa3-institutional-endowment-model", title: "The Endowment Model & Spending", content: `Endowments and foundations support an institution's mission **in perpetuity**, giving them a long horizon and high risk tolerance.

## The endowment model
- Heavy allocation to **alternatives** (private equity, hedge funds, real assets) to earn an illiquidity premium.
- Requires strong governance and the ability to bear illiquidity.

## Spending rules
- **Constant growth**: prior spending grown by inflation.
- **Market-value rule**: a fixed % of a moving average of asset values (smooths spending).
- **Hybrid (Yale) rule**: a weighted blend of the two, balancing stability and responsiveness.

## Objectives
- Maintain **intergenerational equity** (real value preserved) while funding current spending.
- Required return ≈ spending rate + inflation + costs.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-institutional-alm-m01", topicId: "cfa3-institutional-alm", title: "Asset-Liability Management Frameworks", content: `**Asset-liability management (ALM)** manages assets in explicit relation to liabilities, central for pensions, insurers and banks.

## Approaches
- **ALM (liability-relative)**: choose assets to hedge or match liabilities (e.g., **immunization**, **cash-flow matching**, LDI).
- **Asset-only** ignores liabilities; ALM is preferred when liabilities are significant and well-defined.

## Institution examples
- **Banks** manage the **duration gap** between assets and deposits.
- **Insurers** match asset duration/cash flows to policy liabilities and consider surrender risk.
- **DB pensions** hedge the **funded-ratio** volatility via LDI.

## Key metrics
- **Surplus** (assets − liabilities) and **funded ratio**; managing **surplus risk** (volatility of the surplus) is the ALM objective.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-m01", topicId: "cfa3-ethics-duties-employers", title: "Duties to Employers (Standard IV)", content: `**Standard IV — Duties to Employers.**

## IV(A) Loyalty
- Act for the employer's benefit; do not deprive it of your skills or divulge confidential information.
- **Leaving a firm**: you may use general knowledge/skills but must not take records, client lists, or solicit clients before departure. Independent practice needs employer consent.

## IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements
- Do not accept gifts/compensation that compete with or create a conflict with the employer's interest **without written consent** from all parties.

## IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors
- Members with supervisory roles must take **reasonable steps to prevent and detect** violations by those under their supervision (adequate compliance systems).

Loyalty duties are balanced against the duty to comply with law and not engage in illegal activity for an employer.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-m01", topicId: "cfa3-ethics-investment-actions", title: "Investment Analysis & Actions (Standard V)", content: `**Standard V — Investment Analysis, Recommendations, and Actions.**

## V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis
- Exercise diligence and have a **reasonable, adequate basis** for analysis and recommendations; verify third-party research and quantitative models.

## V(B) Communication with Clients
- Disclose the **basic format and general principles** of the investment process; distinguish **fact from opinion**; identify **material limitations and risks**.

## V(C) Record Retention
- Maintain records supporting analysis and recommendations (recommended **7 years**); records are the employer's property.

Violations often involve acting on inadequate research or presenting opinion as fact.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-m01", topicId: "cfa3-ethics-gips-verification", title: "GIPS Verification & Compliance", content: `**Verification** is a third-party review testing whether a firm's **processes** comply with GIPS.

## What verification does
- Confirms the firm has followed GIPS **on a firm-wide basis** for composite construction and calculation.
- Verification applies to the **whole firm**, not a single composite; a firm cannot claim a specific composite is 'verified' alone.
- Verification is **voluntary but recommended**; it adds credibility and cannot be partial.

## Compliance basics
- Claiming compliance requires meeting **all** requirements — no partial compliance.
- Firms must show a compliant track record (5 years, building to 10) and use the official compliance statement.

Verification does not guarantee the accuracy of any specific composite presentation, only that firm-wide processes meet the standards.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-m01", topicId: "cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees", title: "Hedge Fund Fees & Funds-of-Funds", content: `Hedge funds typically charge **management + incentive fees**, classically **'2 and 20'**.

## Fee mechanics
- Incentive (performance) fees often apply above a **hurdle rate** and subject to a **high-water mark** (no incentive fee until prior losses are recovered).
- Fees materially reduce net returns; compounding of fees matters over time.

## Funds-of-funds
- Provide **diversification** and **due-diligence/access**, but add a **second layer** of fees (e.g., '1 and 10' on top).
- Can suffer from **over-diversification** (diluting alpha) and still bear underlying-fund fees.

## Netting risk
- In multi-manager funds, investors may pay incentive fees to winning managers even if the fund overall is flat (**netting risk**), unless there is a fund-level netting arrangement.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-m01", topicId: "cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence", title: "Alternatives Due Diligence", content: `Due diligence on alternative managers spans **investment** and **operational** review.

## Investment due diligence
- Strategy, edge, and **repeatability** of returns; risk management; use of leverage; capacity constraints.
- Analyze the **return drivers** and whether performance is skill or beta.

## Operational due diligence (ODD)
- **Independent** administrator, custodian and auditor; valuation policy for illiquid assets.
- Controls, compliance, key-person risk, and **business continuity**.
- Operational failures (fraud, weak controls) are a leading cause of hedge-fund losses.

## Terms
- **Lockups, gates, redemption** frequency and notice; fee structure and alignment.

Robust ODD is as important as investment analysis for alternatives.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-m01", topicId: "cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return", title: "Risk-Return & Diversification of Alternatives", content: `Alternatives are added for **return enhancement**, **diversification**, or both — but their statistics can mislead.

## Measurement issues
- Reported returns are often **smoothed** (appraisal-based), understating volatility and correlation and overstating Sharpe ratios.
- Returns show **negative skew and fat tails** (e.g., many strategies resemble selling insurance).
- **Illiquidity** and **survivorship/backfill bias** in indexes flatter historical results.

## Portfolio role
- Assess **correlation in stress**, which often rises, reducing diversification just when needed.
- Use **downside measures** (Sortino, max drawdown, CVaR) rather than Sharpe alone.
- The **illiquidity premium** is compensation, not free return; size allocations to the ability to bear illiquidity.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-m01", topicId: "cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd", title: "Operational Due Diligence", content: `**Operational due diligence (ODD)** evaluates a manager's business and controls, separate from investment merit.

## Focus areas
- **Independence** of key service providers: administrator, custodian, auditor.
- **Valuation** policy (especially for illiquid assets) and pricing sources.
- **Compliance, controls, and cybersecurity**; segregation of duties.
- **Key-person risk** and succession; business viability and AUM stability.

## Red flags
- Self-administration and self-custody, unqualified or tiny auditors, opaque valuation, high staff turnover, past regulatory issues.

Many manager failures are **operational, not investment-driven**; ODD can be a veto over an otherwise attractive strategy.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-m01", topicId: "cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors", title: "Type I & II Errors in Selection", content: `Manager selection decisions carry two error types.

## The errors
- **Type I error**: **hiring/keeping a manager who adds no value** (rejecting the null 'no skill' when it's true — a false positive).
- **Type II error**: **rejecting/firing a manager who does add value** (a false negative).

## Implications
- Chasing recent outperformance raises Type I errors (paying for luck).
- Firing after short-term underperformance raises Type II errors (dropping skilled managers at the wrong time).
- A robust process weighs **statistical significance** of past returns against costs of switching.

Because performance is noisy, both errors are common; disciplined, evidence-based processes reduce them.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 },
  { id: "cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-m01", topicId: "cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios", title: "Capture Ratios & Manager Metrics", content: `**Capture ratios** describe how a manager performs in up versus down markets.

## Definitions
- **Upside capture**: manager return ÷ benchmark return in **up** markets (>100% is good).
- **Downside capture**: manager return ÷ benchmark return in **down** markets (<100% is good).
- **Capture ratio** = upside capture ÷ downside capture; >1 indicates a favorable asymmetry.

## Other selection metrics
- **Batting average** (fraction of periods beating the benchmark) and **up/down number ratios**.
- **Drawdown** statistics and **consistency** of the information ratio.

These metrics reveal a manager's **convexity** — the ideal is to capture more of the upside than the downside.`, estimatedReadTime: 2, orderIndex: 0 }
];

